FEATURE: modal-stacking
DATE: 2026-08-03
SCOPE: add real modal stacking to ModalDisplayer
FOLLOWS: changelog/modal-displayer-verification

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected. No other project in the
workspace consumes these files, and there is no build step or dependency graph to
propagate through; changes take effect on the next page load.

Consumer-visible surface:
  - vUX-modalDisplayer.js         restructured internally; two members added
  - assets/css/modalDisplayer.css one selector widened

Public API is additive: closeAll() and depth are new, and no existing member was
renamed or removed. Two behaviour changes do affect existing consumers — see
BEHAVIOUR CHANGES below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT WAS BUILT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A trigger inside a displayed modal now opens a new modal on top of it. The modal
underneath stays open and intact, and closing the top one reveals it again.

Design, and why it is shaped this way:

1. One overlay per layer, created on open and removed on close, each with its own
   z-index, its own responsive width and its own scroll container. A single shared
   overlay cannot represent two modals — it has one backdrop and one scroll box.

2. All per-modal state moved from closure variables onto a layer object held in an
   array. Overwriting those variables was the mechanism by which nesting destroyed
   the first modal.

3. Nothing is addressed by global id any more. Each layer holds direct node
   references and finds its animation boxes by class, scoped to itself. Duplicate
   ids across layers were what made close() restore the wrong element.

4. Page-level state is handled at the stack boundary. The body freeze and scroll
   capture happen when the stack becomes non-empty and are released when it empties,
   so nesting cannot re-capture a already-frozen body.

5. The stack is module scoped and shared by every ModalDisplayer instance, because
   the state it protects — the body freeze, the scroll position — belongs to the
   document. A stack per instance would let two instances independently freeze and
   unfreeze the body, recreating the bug this change fixes. Instance configuration
   is snapshotted onto each layer at open time, so a layer always closes with the
   settings and callbacks of the instance that opened it.

6. Event handlers that act on the stack (Escape, away click, close button,
   transitionend, resize) are bound once for the document rather than once per
   instance; otherwise two instances would pop two layers for one Escape press.
   Opening stays per instance, since each recognises its own trigger class.

7. Layers close last-in-first-out, enforced rather than merely offered. This is the
   defining property of a modal stack, not a restriction on it: a modal takes
   exclusive control of interaction, so a covered layer is unreachable by
   definition and has no user-reachable way to be dismissed. Closing one out of
   order would leave the dialog it spawned floating above nothing. Every close path
   (close(), Escape, away click, close button) therefore acts on the topmost layer,
   and no API accepts a specific layer. closeAll() exists for dismissing the whole
   stack at once, top down.

Refused rather than allowed to corrupt state: stacking a modal on itself, and
triggers pointing at a modal that is already displayed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUGS FIXED ALONG THE WAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Nested open destroyed the first modal and froze the page permanently.
   Reproduced before the change: after opening a modal from inside another and
   closing, document.getElementById("loginModal") returned null, its inputs were
   gone, an element was stranded as id="vOld", two #newModal elements remained in
   the overlay, the scroll position was lost, and body stayed position:fixed so the
   page could never be scrolled again. Nothing threw — the failure was silent.

2. openProcessor fired twice for the "box" effect. Its animation box uses
   `transition: all`, animating width and height, so transitionend fired once per
   property and the completion logic ran twice. Measured per effect before the
   change: none 1, split 1, flip 1, box 2. Completion is now idempotent per layer.

3. Responsive widths never applied on open with the default "none" effect. The
   width was only set from the transitionend and resize handlers, neither of which
   the "none" effect reaches, leaving config.modalWidths, modalWidthsAttribute and
   screenBreakPoints inert unless the user resized the window.

4. Consumer transitions inside a modal could drive the open/close state machine.
   The old transitionend handler matched on the event target's parent classes, so
   any animated element inside a modal could trigger it. It now requires one of the
   library's own animation boxes.

5. backface-visibility was misspelled "backface-visibilty" in the flip effect's
   inline CSS, so no browser applied it and the reverse face was not hidden.

6. Closing wrote position:static onto the body even when the page had no inline
   position, because the restore value was read from computed style. It now captures
   and restores the inline value.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BEHAVIOUR CHANGES (existing consumers should read this)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Modals opened with effect "none" are now sized by the responsive width triple
   (default 500px / 500px / 86%) instead of by their own content width. This is the
   fix described in bug 3 above. Consumers relying on content-width sizing with the
   default effect should set config.modalWidths, or the per-trigger widths
   attribute, to their previous size.

2. No overlay exists in the DOM until the first modal opens. Previously one was
   built at initialize() and stayed in the document. Code that queried .vModal or
   [data-id='vModalStyles'] at startup will now find nothing until a modal is open.

Backward compatibility retained deliberately: the first layer still carries
data-id="vModalStyles" on its overlay and id="newModal" on its content host, so
selectors written against the pre-stacking DOM keep working for single-modal use.
Layers above the first omit them, since duplicate ids are the defect being fixed.
New code should target the .vModalHost class.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Driven in headless Chromium against the example page served over HTTP from the
repository root, asserting DOM state, computed styles, callback counts, z-index
ordering, id uniqueness, body freeze state and scroll position.

  Main suite            68 passed, 0 failed
  Stacking edge suite   16 passed, 0 failed

The main suite carries forward every check from the previous feature (all four
effects opening and closing, content restoration, scroll preservation, tall modals,
per-trigger widths, blur) and adds stacking coverage: two layers open together, both
modals visible, z-index ordering, no duplicate ids, getters following the top layer,
Escape popping exactly one layer, the lower modal surviving intact, the page staying
frozen while any layer is up, closeAll(), the self-stacking guard, and two instances
sharing one stack with one Escape popping one layer.

The edge suite covers three-deep nesting with per-level unwinding and scroll
restoration, blur reference behaviour across layers (not released while a layer
still wants it), and a close button popping only its own layer.

Callback counts were re-measured per effect after the change: all four effects now
report exactly one openProcessor and one closeProcessor call per cycle.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KNOWN LIMITATIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- closeAll() unwinds without animation, since animating several layers out at once
  has no coherent visual result.
- Modal content is still copied via innerHTML, so listeners bound directly to nodes
  inside the content do not survive an open/close cycle. Consumers should delegate,
  or bind through displayForm on open. Unchanged by this work and documented.
- While the body is frozen it is position:fixed without an explicit width, so a page
  whose layout depends on body width may reflow behind the overlay. Pre-existing and
  left alone to avoid changing layout for existing consumers.
